  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 119 AM MST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nWednesday begins the long term period with a modest front backing \nsouthwestward thru eastern NM. Aside from a modest northeasterly \nwind shift a slight drop in temperatures, there will be little \nsensible weather changes from this front. Meanwhile, the H5 ridge \ncontinues to amplify over the Desert Southwest and Four Corners \nregion. This ridge remains highly amplified and sets up temporary \nresidence over the region through Friday, keeping calm and pleasant \nweather over New Mexico. \n\nLate Friday into Friday night sees the weather pattern finally \nchange with two approaching storm systems from the Pacific. \nDeterministic and ensemble means are in rather good agreement with \nthe first upper level storm system opening up and tracking south of \nNM over northern Mexico. This leaves the best chance for \nprecipitation coming from the 2nd storm system progged to track over \nthe northern half of NM Saturday and Saturday night. Much of the \nnorthern mountains look to fair well with valley rain and high \nelevation snow, favoring the west slopes. Windy conditions look to \naccompany this storm system as well, aided by 40-45kt H7 winds and a \nlee-side surface low deepening over southeastern CO. Crosswinds \nfavoring gusts upwards of 35-50 mph will be favored on north-to-\nsouth oriented highways, notably along and just east of the central \nmountain chain.\n\nMonday sees a quick break with a ridge of high pressure sliding west \nto east across the region. A more active northern Pacific looks to \nbring in another set of storm systems into the western CONUS with \nthe next favoring to bring more notable precipitation to New Mexico \nTuesday and Wednesday of next week.",
